WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.420
Browser-facing ASP.NET Core applications need defenses that match how browsers send requests and handle content.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:15.560
CSRF risk appears when a browser can send an authenticated state-changing request because a user is already signed in.

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:28.940
Antiforgery protections matter most in flows where cookies authenticate the request and the operation changes server-side state, such as form submissions or actions that modify records.

4
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.180
Antiforgery protection should be applied intentionally.

5
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:43.640
The goal is not to decorate every endpoint blindly, but to understand which flows rely on browser cookies, which methods change state, and where framework antiforgery patterns should be used.

6
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:52.300
API designs that use different authentication patterns may have different needs, but state-changing browser flows should not be left to chance.

7
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.220
CORS is often misunderstood.

8
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:03.640
It is a browser-enforced sharing policy that controls whether browser-based scripts from one origin can read responses from another origin.

9
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.260
It is not a general server-side authorization mechanism.

10
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:15.600
A server still has to authenticate the caller, authorize the operation, validate input, and protect data even if CORS is configured correctly.

11
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.200
Avoid overly broad CORS settings.

12
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:24.940
Broad origins, methods, headers, or credential settings can make browser exposure wider than intended.

13
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.880
CORS rules should reflect real client applications, deployment environments, and data sensitivity.

14
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:36.260
Temporary settings used during development should not drift into production as permanent policy.

15
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.780
Cookie attributes and security headers support safer browser behavior.

16
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:47.280
Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite settings reduce common exposure paths for cookies.

17
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:55.700
Headers that control framing, content type handling, transport expectations, referrer behavior, and content execution can limit accidental browser trust.

18
00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.320
These settings are supporting controls, not a substitute for server-side authorization and validation.

19
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.360
Client-side controls are useful, but they are not enforcement.

20
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:16.820
A user interface can hide actions, validate fields, and guide behavior, but server-side code must still protect state-changing operations, sensitive data, and privileged workflows.

21
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.920
Browser defenses work best when they reinforce a server design that is already explicit about trust.
